  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Linearity of the Laplace Transform",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply linearity to convert scaled sums of causal time-domain signals into matching scaled sums of known one-sided Laplace transform pairs, setting up algebraic simplification in the s-domain.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Linearity of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Linearity of the Laplace Transform",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The move in this step is one-sided linearity: if signals are causal, then algebraic operations in time carry directly into the transform domain with the same scalar and sum structure. A causal signal satisfies $x(t)=0$ for $t<0$, so the one-sided transform operator $\\mathcal{L}_+$ is the one used in this topic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the learning goal by starting with the base case. For a known causal transform pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(s)$, multiply the time signal by a scalar $a$ and carry that coefficient outside the transform operator before doing any further expansion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{a x(t)\\} = aX(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next, add the two-term sum rule. For causal signals, add transform-domain terms term-by-term in the same order so each original signal keeps its own transformed identity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x_1(t)+x_2(t)\\} = X_1(s) + X_2(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Combine both ideas into a single operational rule and keep a short notation checklist: pull out coefficients, match each time-domain term, then write the transform sum with the same coefficients and signs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{a x_1(t)+b x_2(t)\\} = aX_1(s)+bX_2(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Linearity of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Scaled Causal Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the one-sided Laplace transform of $x(t)=3e^{-2t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: identify the expression as a single causal exponential scaled by a constant. Write $x(t)=3\\,e^{-2t}u(t)$ as a scalar multiple of the base pair $e^{-2t}u(t)$, where $u(t)$ confirms one-sided causality.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+2},\\quad \\Re\\{s\\}>-2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use linearity to pull the coefficient $3$ outside the one-sided transform operator first, then apply the matched pair without introducing any additional transform-algebra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{3e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=3\\,\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=3\\left(\\frac{1}{s+2}\\right).",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: $X(s)=\\dfrac{3}{s+2}$, with causality preserved by the one-sided transform context.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Linearity of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Sum of Causal Signals",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)+e^{-3t}u(t)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: the input is a sum of causal exponentials, so match each term independently before applying linearity to the whole sum. The structure is $x_1(t)+x_2(t)$ with $x_1(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ and $x_2(t)=e^{-3t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+1},\\quad \\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-3t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now choose the linearity rule for sums: transform the whole expression as the sum of transformed terms, keeping the same ordering and no coefficient changes because both terms are unit-scaled.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)+e^{-3t}u(t)\\}=\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)\\}+\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-3t}u(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)+e^{-3t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result (kept separated for the next step): $X(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+3}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Linearity of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Scaled Sum of Causal Signals",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Transform $x(t)=4e^{-t}u(t)-\\frac{1}{2}e^{-4t}u(t)$ using linearity.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: this signal already has two causal terms, each with its own scalar, and opposite signs in the sum. Write it as two scalar-weighted components $4x_1(t)-\\frac{1}{2}x_2(t)$ so both the previous rules apply together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Define the core pieces: $x_1(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ and $x_2(t)=e^{-4t}u(t)$. Then use the known one-sided pairs for each piece.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x_1(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+1},\\quad \\mathcal{L}_+\\{x_2(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now split and transform with linearity in one pass, preserving the exact coefficient sign and value on each matched term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{4e^{-t}u(t)-\\frac{1}{2}e^{-4t}u(t)\\}=4\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)\\}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-4t}u(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{4e^{-t}u(t)-\\frac{1}{2}e^{-4t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{4}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s+4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $X(s)=\\dfrac{4}{s+1}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{s+4}$, with each coefficient and sign kept explicit before later simplification steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Linearity of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Simplifying the Resulting Expression in the s-Domain",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Transform $x(t)=3e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-3t}u(t)$ and simplify $\\frac{3}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+3}$ to a single fraction.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: the step already has the linearity-mapped split form. Keep the termwise matches from previous steps and use the added algebraic step: combine $\\frac{3}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+3}$ into one ratio.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First restate the linearity result from the time-domain side and keep the two terms identified before simplifying:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{3e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-3t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now perform common-denominator combining. The shared denominator is $(s+1)(s+3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{3}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+3}=\\frac{3(s+3)}{(s+1)(s+3)}-\\frac{(s+1)}{(s+1)(s+3)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{3(s+3)}{(s+1)(s+3)}-\\frac{(s+1)}{(s+1)(s+3)}=\\frac{2s+8}{(s+1)(s+3)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final simplified result: $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}=\\frac{2s+8}{(s+1)(s+3)}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
